// The shell's defaults: Inspo's own tally test on the sibling site. A customer
// lane builds with its own xcconfig (xcodebuild -xcconfig <lane>/deploy/ios.xcconfig),
// which sets these four; nothing of a customer is in this repository.
// (In an xcconfig // starts a comment, so a URL is written https:/$()/...)
INSPO_URL = https:/$()/client-inspo.web.app/tally
INSPO_APP_NAME = Inspo
INSPO_BUNDLE_ID = com.inspoui.shell
INSPO_URL_SCHEME = inspo
// A test build's stand-in for a native library (E42): the library's name
// (id-reader) puts StandInReader in its place; empty, none.
INSPO_NATIVE_STAND_IN =
// The Bluetooth ID scanner a customer build links (E47): the library's name
// in its urls (id-reader). Empty here: the vendor's package is never in this
// repository; a customer's Xcode workspace links it (README) and sets this.
INSPO_ID_SCANNER =
// Push (README, "Push"). A build that is to have a push token is signed
// with the push entitlement: its lane's xcconfig names the file,
// CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS = <this repository>/Config/Push.entitlements, which
// asks Apple's push environment below and time-sensitive notifications.
// Not named here: a build that does not name it is signed as before, and its
// token call fails (Apple registers no app without the entitlement).
// development for every build signed on a Mac: one built for a simulator,
// one run from Xcode on a phone, and the archive too. An archive is signed
// with the team's development profile, whose push environment is
// development, and an entitlements file that asks production does not match
// it: Xcode stops the archive ("Provisioning profile ... doesn't match the
// entitlements file's value for the aps-environment entitlement"). The
// upload signs the archive again for distribution and writes production by
// itself (scripts/upload.sh), so a build from TestFlight or the App Store is
// production with nothing set here. A lane leaves this as it is.
INSPO_PUSH_ENVIRONMENT = development
